            <ArticleTitle>A Review of Atypical Femoral Fractures From a Tertiary Care Teaching Hospital: An Alarming Trend?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>182-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BOT.0000000000000539</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the experience of a single tertiary care teaching hospital with the association between use of bisphosphonates and atypical femoral fractures, and to identify potential risk factors and chronologic incidence.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary-care teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-two patients with a total of 43 fractures met inclusion criteria.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Treatment for atypical femur fractures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Radiographic assessments, duration of bisphosphonate therapy, prodromal symptoms, associated medications/comorbidities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Thirty-one female patients and 1 male patient, with a total of 43 fractures, with an average age of 71.0 (47-92 years) ± 11.01 years at time of fracture, had an average duration of bisphosphonate therapy of 7.57 (1-12 years) ± 3.07 years. Prodromal thigh pain was reported in 20 of 43 fractures (46.5%). Eleven of the forty-three (25.5%) fractures occurred between 2001 and 2005. From 2006 to May 2011, however, 32/43 (74.5%) were reported. Beta-blockers, ACE/ARBs, and Statins (43.8%) were the most common concomitant medications, whereas Levothyroxine (21.8%), H-2 blockers (15.6%), and Glucocorticoids (12.5%) were less common.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There is an increasing trend of atypical femur fractures in the current adult population. Bisphosphonate therapy beyond 7 years may place patients at increased risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prognostic Level IV. See Instructions for Authors for a complete description of levels of evidence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
